   },
   {
     "heading": "10. Analytics, diagnostics, device identifiers, and app health",
-    "body": "We use Firebase Analytics, Firebase Crashlytics, PostHog where enabled, and our own limited app activity/error logs to operate, secure, debug, measure, and improve the App. These records may include app events, screens/features used, purchase/paywall/referral events, AI feature events, onboarding choices, notification interactions, message or survey interactions, error context, crash logs, stack traces, app version, build number, platform, OS version, device name or model, app state, user name, Firebase/Auth IDs, stable app ID, app instance identifiers, PostHog distinct IDs where enabled, and other app metadata.\n\nThese records are not described as anonymous. Some diagnostics and analytics may be linked to your app account, Firebase/Auth ID, stable app ID, app instance, or device identifiers.\n\nWhen the support diagnostics feature is enabled, the App keeps a bounded technical action history on your device (for example which screens or actions were used and technical error codes, a limited number of recent entries). This history is sent to Knowly only when you explicitly submit an in-app support report, and is attached to that report to help us investigate the problem. It does not include message text, passwords, payment details, authentication tokens, or purchase receipts. Support reports and their attached history are retained only as long as needed to resolve the issue and keep reasonable support and security records.\n\nWhen you send an in-app report about a problem, we receive your comment, the screen and content item it concerns, the answer you gave (if any), your nickname and interface language, basic account information (such as level, streak, Premium status, and days in the App), and device information (device model, operating system and version, and screen size). If support diagnostics are enabled, the technical action history described above is attached.\n\nWe ask for your consent before collecting non-essential product analytics (such as PostHog analytics and non-essential Firebase Analytics events). If you decline, or later withdraw consent in the App, we do not collect that non-essential analytics and do not link it to your account or identifiers. We still process a limited amount of strictly necessary information, such as crash and error diagnostics, security, and fraud-prevention signals, which we rely on to keep the App working and secure. You can change your analytics choice at any time in the App settings.\n\nThe App does not use the Google Play Services Advertising ID and does not request it. We do not sell personal information, and we do not use any advertising identifier for third-party advertising or cross-context behavioral advertising unless this Policy, store disclosures, and any required consent are updated first."
+    "body": "We use Firebase Analytics, Firebase Crashlytics, PostHog where enabled, and our own limited app activity/error logs to operate, secure, debug, measure, and improve the App. These records may include app events, screens/features used, purchase/paywall/referral events, AI feature events, onboarding choices, notification interactions, message or survey interactions, error context, crash logs, stack traces, app version, build number, platform, OS version, device name or model, app state, user name, Firebase/Auth IDs, stable app ID, app instance identifiers, PostHog distinct IDs where enabled, and other app metadata.\n\nThese records are not described as anonymous. Some diagnostics and analytics may be linked to your app account, Firebase/Auth ID, stable app ID, app instance, or device identifiers.\n\nWhen the support diagnostics feature is enabled, the App keeps a bounded technical action history on your device (for example which screens or actions were used and technical error codes, a limited number of recent entries). This history is sent to Knowly only when you explicitly submit an in-app support report, and is attached to that report to help us investigate the problem. It does not include message text, passwords, payment details, authentication tokens, or purchase receipts. Support reports and their attached history are retained only as long as needed to resolve the issue and keep reasonable support and security records.\n\nWhen you send an in-app report about a problem, we receive your comment, the screen and content item it concerns, the answer you gave (if any), your nickname and interface language, basic account information (such as level, streak, Premium status, and days in the App), and device information (device model, operating system and version, and screen size). If support diagnostics are enabled, the technical action history described above is attached.\n\nWe ask for your consent before collecting non-essential product analytics (such as PostHog analytics and non-essential Firebase Analytics events). If you decline, or later withdraw consent in the App, we do not collect that non-essential analytics and do not link it to your account or identifiers. We still process a limited amount of strictly necessary information, such as crash and error diagnostics, security, and fraud-prevention signals, which we rely on to keep the App working and secure. You can change your analytics choice at any time in the App settings. We also keep a record of your analytics and AI-feature consent choices (granted or denied, when, app version and platform) so we can honour and demonstrate them.\n\nThe App does not use the Google Play Services Advertising ID and does not request it. We do not sell personal information, and we do not use any advertising identifier for third-party advertising or cross-context behavioral advertising unless this Policy, store disclosures, and any required consent are updated first."
   },
   {
     "heading": "11. AI features and OpenAI processing",
@@ -53,7 +53,7 @@
   },
   {
     "heading": "13. Text-to-speech, speech recognition, and voice features",
-    "body": "The App uses your device's built-in text-to-speech engine through expo-speech to pronounce English phrases and example sentences. Based on the current implementation, pronunciation is generated on device and Knowly does not receive the generated text-to-speech audio.\n\nWhere speech recognition, pronunciation practice, or voice dialogue features are enabled, the App may request microphone and speech recognition permissions. Depending on the feature, speech may be processed on device or by platform speech services, and a transcript or recognized text may be used by the App. If you use an AI voice dialogue feature, the transcript or recognized text may be sent to the AI provider as described in the AI features section. Do not say sensitive personal, confidential, payment, medical, legal, financial, or emergency information in voice features.\n\nSome of the libraries that power these voice features are general-purpose media and animation libraries that also support video capture and motion sensors. Because of this, on some platforms the operating system may list camera or motion permissions for the App. The App does not take photos, record video, open the camera, or read motion and fitness sensors, and it never asks you for those permissions; the only sensor input the App captures is microphone audio for the voice features described above."
+    "body": "The App uses your device's built-in text-to-speech engine through expo-speech to pronounce English phrases and example sentences. Based on the current implementation, this pronunciation is generated on device and Knowly does not receive the generated text-to-speech audio. Some approved Community Packs also include pre-generated audio for their cards, created on our servers from the card text by our AI provider's text-to-speech service, as described in the Community Packs section; it does not use your voice or anything you type.\n\nWhere speech recognition, pronunciation practice, or voice dialogue features are enabled, the App may request microphone and speech recognition permissions. Depending on the feature, speech may be processed on device or by platform speech services, and a transcript or recognized text may be used by the App. If you use an AI voice dialogue feature, the transcript or recognized text may be sent to the AI provider as described in the AI features section. Do not say sensitive personal, confidential, payment, medical, legal, financial, or emergency information in voice features.\n\nSome of the libraries that power these voice features are general-purpose media and animation libraries that also support video capture and motion sensors. Because of this, on some platforms the operating system may list camera or motion permissions for the App. The App does not take photos, record video, open the camera, or read motion and fitness sensors, and it never asks you for those permissions; the only sensor input the App captures is microphone audio for the voice features described above."
   },
